 [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/0ae64bf57444ac42ec5efed392471cbbac7b1e317bc1fb06b33bcd2038a2e3c1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10\\pi "] ["math" "-5\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10\\pi "] ["math" "5\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\pi "] ["math" "-5\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "5\\pi "] ["math" "9\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15\\pi "] ["math" "-4\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "6\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "3\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\pi \\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\pi "] ["math" "-8\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "8\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+3x)\\,i+(2xy+3)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+y.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement III is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "q-125650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "07ac6e16e9b1dbfaf7336b11f1754dd3b4a5e0b286fe0bd91a2cf6479cb3d5dd"} {"question" "e-9537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9537.json" "file_sha256" "bc3bb63464711b3236945d75433dc0e63e9c312be52fefc0274d0c8599d3d0cd"} {"question" "e-9537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9537.json" "file_sha256" "bc3bb63464711b3236945d75433dc0e63e9c312be52fefc0274d0c8599d3d0cd"} {"question" "e-9539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9539.json" "file_sha256" "54173a4cbe5e79468e383fe317000459ce090f2dc7bceae53c1986bda9650d75"} {"question" "e-9539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9539.json" "file_sha256" "54173a4cbe5e79468e383fe317000459ce090f2dc7bceae53c1986bda9650d75"} {"question" "e-9543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9543.json" "file_sha256" "53abc7337c5d369365f329feb2bcf172fdbb366ac189ad4dcd4e2fb09b567328"} {"question" "e-9543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/example-9543.json" "file_sha256" "53abc7337c5d369365f329feb2bcf172fdbb366ac189ad4dcd4e2fb09b567328"}] "usage" {"e-9543" [] "e-9537" [] "e-9539" [] "q-125485" [] "q-125395" [] "q-125424" []} "basis" 1935 "retractions" [[17592186179954 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/q-125395.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F$, shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr = 0$\n3. $∮_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$"] [17592186179959 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x, y) = \\frac{5}{y}i + \\frac{1}{x}j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25x^{2} + y^{2} = 25$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186179957 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y) = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∇f$ is conservative\n2. $∮_(C)∇f \\cdot dr = 0$ for any closed path $C ∈ R^{2}$\n3. $∫_(C)∇f \\cdot dr = 0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C ∈ R^{2}$"] [17592186179532 :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$, shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/611bd674f62d8f3f4a37069e92e426cd.png)\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$"] [17592186179532 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$.\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C$, it neither helps nor hinders its motion.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/84d5f3fc4f5cfdf0b01cdab07f394220.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C$, then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/6155f43399f2bd6f77151753d036ffa3.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186179533 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186179533 :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = \\sin t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nF(r(t)) =-yi + xj =-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&= \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ = ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}dt \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179535 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =(e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$"] [17592186179535 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$, then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$, that is\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0\n$$\n\nWe notice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = e^y\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [1